# Tutorial 11 — Subroutines and Flow
# How to organize scripts with labels, calls, returns, and cross-file navigation.

@scenario {{SCENARIO_PREFIX}}_tutorial_11_subroutines
@include {{CHARACTERS_INCLUDE}}
{{BG_DECL}}@label start
{{BG_START}}[inc tutorial_count]
[textspeed 28]
[autodelay 1650]
[show lavender center happy]
[wait 200]
Lavender: Scripts get long fast. The question is: how do you keep them organized?
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Labels, jumps, calls, and returns — these are your tools for structuring flow without turning your script into spaghetti.

@label lesson_menu
[show lavender far_left talking]
Lavender: What would you like to learn about?
> Labels as structure -> section_labels
> Call and return -> section_call
> Include files -> section_include
> Organizing large scripts -> section_organizing
> Quick reference card -> reference_sheet
> Practice scene -> practice_scene
> Back to Tutorial Hub -> return_hub

# ─── Labels as Structure ─────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_labels
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: You've been using labels since Tutorial 01 — every {b}@label{/b} is a named anchor point in your script.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}@label morning_routine{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}Lavender: Good morning! Time to start the day.{/color}
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Labels aren't just for choices. Think of them as chapters, scenes, or reusable blocks.
Lavender: Good label names describe {i}what happens{/i} there:
narrator: {b}Good:{/b} morning_routine, confrontation, rooftop_ending
narrator: {b}Bad:{/b} label1, scene_a, thing
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: When your script has 50+ labels, naming is the difference between "easy to navigate" and "nightmare."
> Next: Call and return -> section_call
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Call and Return ─────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_call
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: {b}[call label]{/b} is like calling a function — it jumps to a label, runs it, and then comes {i}back{/i} to where you were.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}Lavender: Let me check something real quick.{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[call check_inventory]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}Lavender: Okay, back. Where were we?{/color}
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: The key is {b}[return]{/b} at the end of the called label:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}@label check_inventory{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}narrator: You rummage through your bag. Nothing useful.{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[return]{/color}
Lavender: Without [return], the script keeps running past that label into whatever comes next — which is almost never what you want.
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: {b}[call]{/b} vs {b}[jump]{/b} — the difference:
narrator: {b}[jump]{/b} moves to a label and {i}stays{/i} there. One-way trip.
narrator: {b}[call]{/b} moves to a label and {i}comes back{/i}. Round trip.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Use [call] for reusable bits — inventory checks, recurring NPC greetings, flashback sequences. Use [jump] for permanent scene changes.
> Next: Include files -> section_include
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Include Files ───────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_include
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: The {b}@include{/b} directive pulls in content from another file — typically character definitions.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}@include characters/main_cast.vns{/color}
Lavender: This is how every tutorial file loads the Lavender character definition. You write it once, include it everywhere.
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: Include files are great for:
narrator: {b}Character declarations{/b} — define names, display colors, and IDs in one place.
narrator: {b}Shared variables{/b} — initialize common state at the start of each file.
narrator: {b}Macros{/b} — recurring setup blocks that multiple scenarios need.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: If you change a character's display name in the include file, it updates everywhere. No search-and-replace across 30 files.
> Next: Organizing -> section_organizing
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Organizing Large Scripts ────────────────────────────────────

@label section_organizing
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: As your game grows, you'll need a strategy for script organization. Here's what works:
narrator: {b}One scenario per file.{/b} Don't put three scenes in one file. Split them.
narrator: {b}Descriptive file names.{/b} ch1_morning.vns, ch1_confrontation.vns, ch1_rooftop.vns.
narrator: {b}Use @scenario IDs.{/b} Each file declares a unique scenario name for [goto] targeting.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: For navigation between files:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[goto ch1_confrontation:start]{/color}
Lavender: The scenario ID comes from the {b}@scenario{/b} line at the top of each file. It's the file's address.
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: Folder structure matters too:
narrator: {b}scripts/chapter_01/{/b} — all scenes for chapter 1.
narrator: {b}scripts/shared/{/b} — include files, utility scripts, common labels.
narrator: {b}scripts/tutorial/{/b} — you're looking at it right now.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: The goal is: any team member can open a file and understand where it fits without reading every other file first.
> Reference card -> reference_sheet
> Try it yourself -> practice_scene
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Reference Card ──────────────────────────────────────────────

@label reference_sheet
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Subroutines and flow cheat sheet:
narrator: {b}@label name{/b} — define a named anchor point.
narrator: {b}[jump label]{/b} — one-way move to a label in the same file.
narrator: {b}[call label]{/b} — jump to a label and come back when it hits [return].
narrator: {b}[return]{/b} — return to the point after the most recent [call].
narrator: {b}[goto scenario:label]{/b} — cross-file navigation to a different scenario.
narrator: {b}@include path{/b} — pull in content from another file.
narrator: {b}@scenario name{/b} — declare this file's scenario ID for [goto] targeting.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: [call] + [return] is your best friend for reusable content. Once you start using it, you'll wonder how you ever managed without it.
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Practice Scene ──────────────────────────────────────────────

@label practice_scene
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: Let's see [call] and [return] in action. I'll run a few subroutines live.
Lavender: First, a greeting:
[call sub_greeting]
Lavender: See? I came back. Now let me check something:
[call sub_check]
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Two subroutine calls, and the flow returned here each time. Clean and predictable.
Lavender: Pick a scenario to see how you'd use this in a real scene:
> A recurring NPC encounter -> practice_npc
> A flashback sequence -> practice_flashback
> A utility check -> practice_utility

@label sub_greeting
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Hello! This is the greeting subroutine. I wave, I smile, and then...
[return]

@label sub_check
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Checking... checking... okay, all clear.
[return]

@label practice_npc
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: Imagine a shopkeeper you visit multiple times. Instead of rewriting the greeting each time:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[call shopkeeper_greeting]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}Lavender: What are you selling today?{/color}
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Same greeting, different conversation after. The [call] handles the boilerplate.
[set practice_result 1]
[jump practice_wrap]

@label practice_flashback
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Flashbacks are perfect for [call]. The player dips into a memory, then returns to the present:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}narrator: Your vision blurs. A memory surfaces...{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[call flashback_childhood]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}narrator: You blink. The present returns.{/color}
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: The flashback label has its own staging, dialogue, and a [return] at the end. Self-contained.
[set practice_result 2]
[jump practice_wrap]

@label practice_utility
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: Utility subroutines run logic without showing dialogue — checking flags, updating counters, gating content:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}@label update_stats{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[if courage >= 3]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[set brave_ending 1]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[endif]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[return]{/color}
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Call it wherever you need a stat check. The logic lives in one place, but you can invoke it from anywhere.
[set practice_result 3]
[jump practice_wrap]

@label practice_wrap
[show lavender center happy]
[if practice_result == 1]
Lavender: Recurring NPCs are the classic use case. Write the greeting once, call it from every visit.
[elif practice_result == 2]
Lavender: Flashbacks keep your timeline clean — the memory exists as a subroutine, not tangled into the main flow.
[else]
Lavender: Utility subroutines are invisible to the player but save you from duplicating logic everywhere.
[endif]
Lavender: Start small — even one [call] in your script makes the structure cleaner.
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

@label return_hub
[goto {{HUB_TARGET}}:topics_page_2]
